    section: "B", questionRef: "2022 HL Q14(a)(i)",
    stem: "The cost of building a house has risen by 30% in 2021. Everything has gone up by a staggering rate, from the price of a bag of cement, to the timber on the roof, to the steel. The cost of building a house in late 2021 was €70,000 more than was originally budgeted.",
    questionText: "Explain the term cost push inflation with reference to the statement above.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"fixed","notation":"3 + 3","answer":null,"ofParts":null,"perPart":null}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Cost push inflation refers to increases in the average price of goods and services caused by increases in the cost of production of the finished goods / where increases in cost of production is passed onto the consumer in the form of higher prices. In the above example as the cost of housing raw materials e.g., cement and timber have all increased, leading to increases in the prices of houses across the country.", marks: 6, contextNote: "3 for the definition and 3 for relating it to the statement: dearer cement and timber pushing up house prices.", openList: true },
    ],
