    schemeCitation: "Marking points quoted from the SEC marking scheme, Economics 2024 Higher Level — © State Examinations Commission.",
    id: "econ-2024-hl-q12-b-iii", topicId: "economics-3-3", conceptId: "effects-of-high-inflation",
    section: "B", questionRef: "2024 HL Q12(b)(iii)",
    questionText: "Outline two possible economic effects of high price inflation on the Irish economy.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"bestNofParts","notation":"2 @ 3","answer":2,"ofParts":6,"perPart":3}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "anyN", verbatim: "An effect of high price inflation — any two", marks: 6, contextNote: "Two effects, 3 marks each.", openList: true, group: {"claimMax":2,"perOption":3,"options":["Loss of purchasing power / fall in living standards - it now requires more money to purchase the same goods/services, meaning the purchasing power of consumers in Ireland has fallen. Therefore they purchase fewer goods than they were previously able to, resulting in a lower standard of living / some businesses, to cut costs may reduce their workforce.","Those on fixed incomes are losing lose out - people who earn fixed incomes (e.g., welfare recipients) are suffering more during this inflationary period because they suffer the greatest loss of purchasing power and their incomes are always trying to catch up.","Possibility of wage demands – workers experiencing a reduction in their standard of living may seek an increase in wages causing jobs losses; increase in prices etc.","Loss of international competitiveness – if the inflation rate is higher than that of our competitors then it may cause exports from Ireland to become too expensive and demand fall.","If tax rates are not indexed to the rate of inflation, the issue of fiscal drag can arise – as wages increase to compensate workers for high inflation, workers may move into the marginal tax bracket, meaning a higher proportion of income is paid in tax. This leads to their income being reduced in real terms as prices continue to rise.","Borrowers may benefit: the real cost of repayments is reduced."]} },
    ],
